<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wu Zixu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 484 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wu Zixu was a minister and general of the State of Wu, originally from Chu, who fled to Wu after the Chu king killed his father and brother in 522 BCE. He helped Helü seize the throne and led the campaign that took the Chu capital Ying in 506 BCE, where tradition says he flogged the corpse of King Ping. He was forced to kill himself in 484 BCE after warning King Fuchai against Yue.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wu-zixu.jpg" alt="Wu Zixu" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wu_Yun_(Wu_Zixu).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wu Zixu, whose given name was Wu Yun, was the second son of Wu She, Grand Tutor to Crown Prince Jian of Chu. When King Ping of Chu kept for himself a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> princess chosen as his son's bride, the corrupt official Fei Wuji persuaded him that Wu She and the prince would rebel, and the king had Wu She executed. Forced to write summoning his sons to the capital, Wu She drew the elder, Wu Shang, to die with him, while Wu Zixu fled. The accounts of his life in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a> and other classics differ, showing the influence of folklore.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Pursued with Prince Jian's son, Prince Sheng, he was sheltered near Zhaoguan, the last pass before Wu, by the physician Donggao Gong; legend says his hair turned white from the strain, and the change helped him escape. In Wu he became adviser to Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guang</a> and helped him assassinate King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> of Wu, after which the prince ruled as King Helü. In 506 BCE he joined the invasion of Chu with Helü, his brother Fugai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-tzu">Sun Tzu</a>. After the victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-boju">Battle of Boju</a> he exhumed King Ping's corpse and gave it 300 lashes, and he was made Duke of Shen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Under King Fuchai he was distrusted, while the minister Bo Pi, bribed by Yue, was heeded. Ordered to kill himself, he asked that his eyes be hung on the city gate so that he could watch Yue take the capital; ten years later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goujian">King Goujian of Yue</a> conquered Wu.</p><p>Wu Zixu became a model of loyalty in Chinese culture and is worshipped in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> as Taoshen, God of the Waves, long held to be the god of the tidal bore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qiantang-river">Qiantang River</a>. Some believe the Dragon Boat Festival first commemorated him rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qu-yuan">Qu Yuan</a>, though his cult as a water deity can be traced only to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. In 2006 a memorial to him was completed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wu Zixu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 506 BCE the State of Wu, advised by the Chu exile <b>Wu Zixu</b>, defeated Chu at Boju and occupied Ying; King Zhao fled, and the state survived only with help from the State of Qin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>With the advisers <b>Wu Zixu</b> and, by tradition, Sun Tzu, King Helü captured the Chu capital Ying in 506 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-wu">State of Wu</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jianli, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-state">Wu</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.82,112.9&amp;year=-494" aria-label="Jianli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Wu Zixu"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4475 4999h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4475 4999h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=29.82,112.9&amp;year=-494">What was here in 494 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
